  "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot {(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x} & =(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/9a249437f97a441c409b17e0f30a59bdcb70df02610f18d9320361d3d0d6708a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ increases. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}=(-\\frac{5}{3})\\cdot 0=0.\n$$",
